From a1e7dc318800f16f3e520b70d45f86b657be62ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Frank Reichenbach <frank.w.reichenbach@web.de>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 10:47:09 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Resolve each URI once under a relative $id with a directory

A relative $id with a directory, such as schemas/item.json, was
applied more than once, so refs below it resolved to
schemas/schemas/item.json. As the root $id, it broke every ref
resolved against it. For a subschema with such an $id that refers to
itself, compile() didn't return. urljoin(uri, uri) returns uri only
for absolute URIs and for paths without a directory, and several
places resolved a URI that was already resolved:

- walk() resolved the root $id against itself, because from_schema()
  already starts the resolver in that scope.
- resolving() resolved the URIs the generator passes, and the
  document URI, against the current scope again.
- generate_ref() and _expand_refs() resolved a $ref that walk() had
  already resolved.
- resolving() walked a subschema with an $id again under its URI and
  registered it once more.

is_resolved() tells which $refs walk() resolved. Callers use those,
and URIs that are already resolved, as they are. Every other $ref is
resolved against the scope as before. walk() also marks every
subschema with an $id as walked.

from_schema() tells the resolver that its base URI is the root $id
(base_uri_is_id), so walk() doesn't apply that $id again. Comparing
the two strings instead would also skip the $id when a caller passes
a retrieval URI that happens to be equal to it. The third parameter
of walk() stays rewrite_refs; id_applied is keyword-only.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (code generation)
Assisted-by: GPT-6.1 Terra (code review)
